<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bibliotheca Corviniana</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1460s–<a href="/years/1490">1490</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Corvina Library</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bibliotheca Corviniana was the library of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, assembled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a> mainly in the 1470s and 1480s. With perhaps two thousand volumes, many of them richly illuminated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> made in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> and Buda, it was one of the largest libraries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. It was scattered after the Ottoman capture of Buda, and over two hundred volumes survive in libraries across the world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bibliotheca-corviniana.jpg" alt="Bibliotheca Corviniana" width="320" height="341" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eusebius Corradus Mediolanensis · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Corvina_Codex_Mathias_rex_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
